xnu 0088: name the failure of VFS_ROOT at boot

bsd_init() mounts the root volume and then asks VFS_ROOT for the root
vnode. hfs_root() resolves that by cat_idlookup() on the root CNID, so a
volume whose volume header is valid but whose catalog cannot answer
(i.e. a case-sensitive HFSX image the reader mishandles, or a catalog
whose first node read fails) surfaces as the same "cannot find root
vnode" panic, with no error number: the errno is what tells the path
apart.

Keep the return value of VFS_ROOT and print it, with the root device,
before the panic. The next boot then says ENOENT (2: the catalog has no
such CNID), EIO (5: the catalog read failed) or EINVAL, which is the
difference between the HFSX comparison path and the UFS read path.

diff --git a/bsd/kern/bsd_init.c b/bsd/kern/bsd_init.c
index f7b45bd..3f74173 100644
--- a/bsd/kern/bsd_init.c
+++ b/bsd/kern/bsd_init.c
@@ -925,7 +925,10 @@ bsd_init(void)
 
 	bsd_init_kprintf("calling VFS_ROOT\n");
 	/* Get the vnode for '/'.  Set fdp->fd_fd.fd_cdir to reference it. */
-	if (VFS_ROOT(mountlist.tqh_first, &init_rootvnode, vfs_context_kernel())) {
+	if ((err = VFS_ROOT(mountlist.tqh_first, &init_rootvnode,
+	    vfs_context_kernel())) != 0) {
+		printf("bsd_init: VFS_ROOT failed, error %d, rootdev %s\n",
+		    err, rootdevice);
 		panic("bsd_init: cannot find root vnode: %s", PE_boot_args());
 	}
 	(void)vnode_ref(init_rootvnode);
